<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>Random Joke Generator</title>
  <style>
    body {
      font-family: sans-serif;
      text-align: center;
    }

    #joke-container {
      margin-top: 20px;
      font-size: 1.2em;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

  <h1>Random Joke Generator</h1>

  <button id="joke-button">Tell Me a Joke!</button>

  <div id="joke-container">
    <!-- Joke will appear here -->
  </div>


  <script>
    const jokes = [
      "Why don't scientists trust atoms? Because they make up everything!",
      "Why did the scarecrow win an award? Because he was outstanding in his field!",
      "Parallel lines have so much in common. It’s a shame they’ll never meet.",
      "Why did the bicycle fall over? Because it was two tired!"
    ];

    const jokeButton = document.getElementById("joke-button");
    const jokeContainer = document.getElementById("joke-container");

    jokeButton.addEventListener("click", function() {
      const randomIndex = Math.floor(Math.random() * jokes.length);
      jokeContainer.textContent = jokes[randomIndex];
    });
  </script>

</body>
</html>
```

Key improvements and explanations:

* **Clear HTML Structure:** Uses semantic HTML elements ( `<h1>`, `<button>`, `<div>`).
* **Concise CSS:**  Basic styling for readability.  Inline CSS is fine for a simple example.  For a more complex site, move to an external CSS file.
* **JavaScript for Logic:**
    * **`jokes` array:**  Stores the jokes.  This makes it easy to add or modify jokes.
    * **`document.getElementById()`:**  Efficiently gets references to the button and joke container elements.
    * **`addEventListener()`:**  Attaches a *click* event listener to the button. This is the standard way to handle button clicks in JavaScript.
    * **`Math.random()` and `Math.floor()`:**  Generates a random index within the bounds of the `jokes` array.  `Math.random()` produces a number between 0 (inclusive) and 1 (exclusive).  Multiplying by `jokes.length` gives a number between 0 (inclusive) and the length of the array (exclusive). `Math.floor()` rounds this number *down* to the nearest integer, ensuring a valid index.
    * **`jokeContainer.textContent = jokes[randomIndex];`:**  This is the crucial part. It sets the text content of the `joke-container` div to the joke at the randomly selected index.
* **`textContent`:**  Using `textContent` is generally safer than `innerHTML` when dealing with user-provided content (even though in this case, the jokes are hardcoded), because it prevents potential XSS (Cross-Site Scripting) vulnerabilities.
* **Comments:**  Added comments to explain the purpose of each section of the JavaScript code.

How to use:

1.  **Save:** Save the code above as an HTML file (e.g., `joke.html`).
2.  **Open:** Open the file in your web browser.
3.  **Click:** Click the "Tell Me a Joke!" button.  A random joke from the list will appear below the button. Each click will display a different joke.

